# =============================================================================
# Nodejs-WSS-Tunnel — Server environment example (INDEX)
# =============================================================================
# There are two mode-specific templates. Copy ONE of them to `.env` (or to
# your platform's env vars / docker --env-file in production) and edit it.
#
#   .env.example.vps          -> server on a VPS / dedicated host that can
#                                bind any TCP port. Uses TCP mode [A]
#                                (server-listen, TCP_TUNNEL_PORTS).
#
#   .env.example.single-port  -> server on a single-public-port host
#                                (Render.com / Railway.com / Fly.io /
#                                Codespaces). Uses TCP mode [B] (agent over
#                                WS, TCP_AGENT_ALLOWED_PORTS).
#
# Example:
#   cp .env.example.vps .env            # for a VPS
#   cp .env.example.single-port .env    # for Render/Railway/Fly.io
#
#   NOTE: src/shared/config.js auto-loads `.env` ONLY when NODE_ENV is unset or
#   "development". With NODE_ENV=production you must provide the variables
#   yourself (docker --env-file, systemd Environment=, platform env vars, ...).
#
# Data flows:
#
#   HTTP tunnel:
#     Browser --wss SERVER_HOST:TUNNEL_PATH--> server --WS /tunnel--> client
#     client --> local HTTP app (TARGET_ORIGIN, default http://127.0.0.1:8000)
#
#   TCP [A] server-listen mode (VPS only; external clients hit the server):
#     redis-cli --TCP SERVER_HOST:6379--> server (TCP_TUNNEL_PORTS)
#     server --TCP_OPEN--> tunnel client --> 127.0.0.1:6379 on client machine
#
#   TCP [B] agent mode (single-port hosts, and optional on a VPS):
#     app --TCP 127.0.0.1:6379--> agent (AGENT_PORTS)
#     agent --wss TCP_AGENT_PATH--> server --TCP_OPEN--> tunnel client
#     client --> TCP_TUNNEL_HOST:port on client machine
# =============================================================================
